{
  "_meta": {
    "purpose": "Ordered per-platform builder phase tables that map `==> ` banner lines to a determinate step-K-of-M progress estimate. Data-driven (not code) so the admin-bff pod-log follower ports the SAME tables + regexes for web progress parity (design 2.9 / spec 10).",
    "source": "The `banner \"...\"` call sites in each build-<platform>-stage4.sh, in source order, PLUS bare `echo \"==> ...\"` banner lines (build-x86-host-stage4.sh emits its five 'Emerge group:' banners that way inside the unquoted CHROOT heredocs, where the host banner() function does not exist). A drift probe (test_progress_parse.py) greps BOTH call-site shapes per builder and asserts the count equals the total number of match patterns here, so a builder banner change fails CI until this file is updated.",
    "emerge_group_banners": "The `Emerge group: <image|tools|dependencies|system|world>` banners are NOT job-ladder boundaries — they tell core/emerge_packages.py which group the following `>>> Emerging (N of M)` package stream belongs to (per-package sub-listing in the GUI). Each is listed here as an ALTERNATE pattern of the phase whose banner precedes it in source order, so the drift probe counts it while the forward-only matcher treats it as a same-phase no-op (a matched phase is never re-entered, so the intra-phase emerge fraction is not reset). tests/build-matrix/check.sh's EGB bucket pins the banner TEXT + per-builder group sets; this table only has to stay count-consistent.",
    "banner_regex": "^==>\\s*(.*)$",
    "ansi_regex": "\\x1b\\[[0-9;]*[A-Za-z]",
    "emerge_regex": ">>> (?:Emerging|Installing) \\((\\d+) of (\\d+)\\)",
    "failure_regex": "^\\s*!!",
    "matching": "A phase is reached when a banner line contains ANY of that phase's `any` substrings. Matching is forward-only (monotonic): the parser advances to the earliest not-yet-passed phase whose pattern matches, so conditional/SKIPPED banners never regress the bar. Alternative banners for the SAME boundary (e.g. 'Installing mesh VPN' vs 'Mesh VPN SKIPPED') are grouped into one phase but counted as distinct patterns for the drift probe.",
    "tracks_purpose": "The sibling `tracks` map covers non-image-build tracks that stream a subprocess line-by-line the SAME way (go-live `plan`, cloud-deploy `plan`/`apply`) but have no build platform to key on. A FLAT track (golive-plan, clouddeploy-plan) uses one phases list for every provider — both drivers' `plan` is a quick, provider-agnostic offline render. A NESTED track (clouddeploy-apply) keys `providers` by cloud-deploy provider id, because the emitted apply.sh genuinely differs per provider (aws-ec2's EBS import-snapshot poll loop vs azure-image's blob upload vs openshift-cnv's oc apply), the same way an image build differs by platform. generic-qcow2 has no apply script (cdp_apply_script returns empty) so it has no entry — track_phases() honestly returns [] for it, staying indeterminate. See core/progress_parse.py:track_phases()."
  },
